## 6\. CLASS ADVISER GRADE CONSOLIDATION MODULE

Develop a dedicated dashboard for the class adviser.

The adviser must be able to access only the sections and learners assigned to them, subject to the existing authorization system.

### A. Adviser dashboard

Display:

- Assigned school year.
- Grade level and section.
- Enrolled learners.
- Assigned subjects or learning areas.
- Subject teachers.
- Expected grade submissions.
- Submitted grades.
- Pending submissions.
- Grades under review.
- Returned submissions.
- Accepted grades.
- Learner report card completion status.

Provide filters for grading period, subject, teacher, and learner.

### B. Grade submission monitoring

For every subject assigned to the section, display the grade submission status.

Example:

| Learning Area | Subject Teacher | First Quarter | Second Quarter | Third Quarter | Fourth Quarter |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Mathematics | Assigned teacher | Accepted | Pending | Pending | Pending |
| English | Assigned teacher | Accepted | Accepted | Pending | Pending |
| Science | Assigned teacher | Submitted | Pending | Pending | Pending |

This table is illustrative. Populate the actual subjects, teachers, and grading periods from the school's official records.

Clearly distinguish between pending, submitted, accepted, and finalized grades.

Do not assume that an unsubmitted grade is zero or that an empty field means the learner failed.

### C. Review and validation

The class adviser must be able to:

- Open a submitted grade summary.
- Review the learner list.
- Check that grades correspond to the correct section and subject.
- Identify missing or inconsistent entries.
- Review applicable grade computation summaries.
- Accept valid submissions.
- Return incomplete or incorrect submissions with comments.
- Track resubmissions.
- View the history of corrections.

The adviser must not directly overwrite a subject teacher's raw assessment scores.

If a grade requires correction, return it to the responsible subject teacher or use an explicitly authorized correction workflow.

Any exceptional administrative override must be permission-controlled and audited.

### D. Consolidation rules

The system must consolidate only grades that have reached the required accepted or approved state.

For each learner, retrieve the correct grade from every applicable learning area and grading period.

Match records using stable learner IDs and valid school-year, section, subject, and enrollment relationships—not learner names alone.

Prevent duplicate subjects and incorrect grade assignments.

The adviser must be able to see which subjects have submitted grades and which remain outstanding before preparing or finalizing the report card.

